The means use thirteen small-batch configurations and eleven full-batch ones (Stella has 999 queries); held-out subsets use nine/seven (\cref{tab:7-scheduling}). At batch 1,000, eight groups give $0.886\times$; worst regret is 1.217 (BGE/256).

Vogue's CV 0.338--0.622 selects four groups at batch 1,000. Ratios span 0.895--1.175; $(n_{\rm probe},\alpha)=(16,16),(16,64),(32,64)$ give 0.955, 0.895, 0.955. CV measures spread, not total work: it enables the 0.97 lead yet overgroups low-probe loads (\cref{sec:7-2}).

Across 250 variant/configuration pairs and 234,320 query comparisons, there are no non-tie primary differences or final-ID-set differences. Tied output order can differ. These are sequential synchronous throughput tests, not open-loop tail-latency tests.

